WEBVTT

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:04.990
Shimano Ryousuke...

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.180
A Japanese foreign exchange student.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.150
Entered the country on June 15, 1939.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.960
Marseilles...

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.170
A one-year stay in France...

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.380
Don't try to force yourself to remember.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.430
I think you have temporary amnesia.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.840
You don't remember anything at all?

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.180
You made the ill-advised decision 
to defy a German soldier.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.390
You don't remember the trouble 
we went through to save you?

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.150
Don't give the enemy any information.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.020
I don't know.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.190
I can't remember anything.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.970
<i>What was that?</i>

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.210
Please, tell me what happened.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.200
This is France, right?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.960
Why are German soldiers here?

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.920
I wish I could forget, too,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.950
that my motherland, France,

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.270
is now occupied by Germany.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.850
<i>As the embers of world war smoldered</i>

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.850
<b>Celebrating
Going off to war</b>

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.850
<i>in autumn of 1937,</i>

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.020
<b>Hisanaga Yasutake</b>

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.020
<i>the Imperial Army</i>

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.500
<i>established a clandestine spy training agency.</i>

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.400
<b>Greater East Asia
Cultural Society</b>

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.900
<i>The histories, names, and even ages</i>

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.670
<i>of the elite men who pass its 
rigorous application process</i>

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.210
<i>are all treated as top-secret information.</i>

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.690
<i>They easily complete training 
exercises that push them</i>

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.770
<i>to their physical and mental limits.</i>

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.540
<i>Under the command of founder 
Lieutenant Colonel Yuuki,</i>

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.130
<i>they operate worldwide, 
within the shadows.</i>

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.870
<i>And so a brand-new intelligence 
agency was created.</i>

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.980
<i>Its name is "D-Agency."</i>

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:35.490
It was June 22, 1940.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.980
Episode 3 - Miscalculation

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.150
With no answer to the advancing 
German forces, France was occupied,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.600
and signed an armistice agreement.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:46.950
This program is a work of fiction. 
The characters, organizations, locations, laws, names, 
etc. being portrayed are unrelated to actual events.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.960
For a long time,

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.360
there were no big problems, even in our town.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.960
Don't get too big for your britches!

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:49.960
But then...

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.070
You miserable Nazi scum!

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.870
It started when an old woman threw a rock

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.510
at a German squadron and 
started yelling at them.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.060
The soldiers ignored her at first,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.970
but things escalated, and eventually,

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.190
they couldn't let it continue any longer.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.850
Just as she was about to be executed...

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.950
It was you who rescued her.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:24.400
That's when we rushed the 
Germans to protect you, but...

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.330
One soldier was waving around a rifle,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.580
and it hit you right in the temple.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:32.340
What's this?

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.750
You look almost annoyed, all of a sudden.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:36.990
Not at all!

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.210
At any rate, thanks for saving me.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.430
Are you really Japanese?

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:44.920
Huh? Why do you ask?

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.980
Right now, you're speaking 
French in a Parisian dialect.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.310
But while you were unconscious,

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.480
you were mumbling in Russian.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.050
<i>Huh? For some reason,</i>

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.770
<i>I feel like I've really screwed up...</i>

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.490
That's not all.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.910
These glasses are cosmetic.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.630
Also, there was cotton stuffed in your mouth.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.260
It got in the way when we tried 
to treat you, and removing it,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.570
along with the glasses, 
changed your appearance.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.610
There's something I thought was strange, too.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.510
While we were carrying him back here,

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.560
he was mumbling numbers.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.430
"Ninety to eight to two," I believe.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.840
Ninety to eight to two?

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.610
What does that mean?

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:38.970
<i>I don't know.</i>

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.140
<i>But I don't think I was 
supposed to tell anyone...</i>

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.880
"A man like that for friends beloved,

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.160
or for his country fears not to perish."

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.170
The words of Horace, the ancient Roman poet.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.850
When you regained consciousness in this room,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.470
you read the words reflected in the mirror

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.400
while still in a daze,
even though they were reversed.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:06.000
Shimano...

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:10.490
You're a hero who saved an 
old Frenchwoman's life.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.150
That's why we put ourselves 
in danger to save you.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.780
But the things you do as 
if they're second nature,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.580
they...

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.030
They make me doubt you're 
just another Japanese man.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.610
Who are you, really?

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:32.620
I'm...

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:33.870
Don't talk.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.270
<i>Stop it...</i>

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.070
<i>Stop it.</i>

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.600
<i>Stop it!</i>

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:44.920
<i>Please, stop!</i>

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:46.630
<i>Don't...</i>

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.620
<i>Don't look at me!</i>

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:01.030
Germans.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:03.070
But why?

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.380
How did they find us?

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.170
We might've been tailed.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.140
This is why I was against bringing him here!

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.750
We were very careful to avoid being followed.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.280
There's no way we were tailed—

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.000
Wait, Shimano! Where are you going?

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.800
They came here for me, right?

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.800
I don't want to cause you any more trouble.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.920
Thank you for helping me.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:32.380
Break through.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:38.100
Death is the worst choice you can make. Live.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.060
Wait!

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:42.620
You're wrong, Shimano.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:46.460
We're the ones who'll be in the 
most trouble if you leave.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:47.820
What do you mean?

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.150
Stop, Alain!

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.550
Remember what the Japanese army is doing!

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.330
Shimano has nothing to do with their army.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.630
Besides, it's not as if France 
and Japan are at war right now.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.200
Marie, what do you think?

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:05.770
Shimano saved one of our people.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.030
And his memory loss doesn't seem to be an act.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.680
Shouldn't we at least fill him in?

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.600
I agree with Alain.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:18.630
Then it's two to one.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:21.810
We're

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.390
with the Résistance.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.930
You're members of a secret

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:35.360
underground opposition to the 
German occupation, you mean?

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.760
Let's sneak out the back.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.670
No.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:52.080
There aren't enough soldiers 
outside to justify all those cars.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.430
There are probably four... no, five more.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.850
It's a trap. Their real plan is to catch

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.430
whoever tries to sneak out the back.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:05.770
<i>Why? How do I know that?</i>

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.420
A trap? Then we have no way out.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.440
We can break through.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:13.940
What do you have here?

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:18.160
Any guns? You're Résistance fighters, right?

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.920
Our goal isn't to engage them in combat.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:24.260
So there's...

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.770
Do you have any weapons or not?

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.500
One handgun.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.340
Jean got it for us.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:33.920
Give it to me, right now.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.750
I think it's broken.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.050
The trigger is jammed.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.010
<i>This is...</i>

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.680
Yeah, something's stuck in there.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:56.870
Anything else we could use as a weapon?

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:59.100
No, nothing else.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:01.070
Nothing but kitchen utensils.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.940
When the fighting starts, make sure 
you stock up on anything white.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:07.640
That's an old French saying.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:11.080
Flour, salt, sugar, and...

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.230
A bellows?

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:12.740
Huh?

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:13.890
Do you have a bellows?

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.740
I think there's an old 
foot bellows in the kitchen.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.590
Bring it, along with the flour.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:20.940
Okay!

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.950
Alain, you keep an eye on things 
through the crack in the door.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.640
Jean, loosen the lightbulbs.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.140
Come out!

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.110
We know you're in there!

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.050
Open the door!

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.690
Come out quietly, for your own sake.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.200
Open up!

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.030
I fixed it.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.950
Now we can fire it at any time.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.080
Thank you...

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.070
What was wrong with it?

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:51.360
Not sure.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.440
I took it apart, then put it 
back together, and it was fixed.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:57.130
Maybe it just wasn't assembled properly.

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:58.760
It's an old gun.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:02.550
Who's going to carry it?

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:03.890
I will.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:07.520
No. I procured it, so I'll carry it.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.880
Marie, can you take it?

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:12.590
Huh?

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.090
Of course, it's not to kill the enemy.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:16.860
It's for your own protection.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:18.190
Is that okay?

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.260
Yes.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.150
Certainly.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.590
You're responsible for 
protecting your own life.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:35.740
What the hell is this?

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:36.910
What's going on?

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.920
Shit, I can't see anything!

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.590
Someone turn on the lights!

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:59.370
What the...

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.430
What is this?

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:03.660
What happened?!

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.120
Now, hurry!

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:17.650
Come on.

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:42.740
Shimano...

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:46.360
Just... Just who the hell are you?

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:50.930
There was nothing in that room you 
could've used to make a bomb.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:53.660
I used the flour you'd stockpiled.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.900
A dust explosion.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.130
It's nothing special, really.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:04.330
Fundamentally, it's not much different
from the coal dust explosions

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:05.960
that sometimes happen in mines.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.660
With the right amount of dust in the air,

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:11.340
balanced with the right density of oxygen,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:12.760
anyone can cause one.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:19.230
Jean, Marie, listen.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:24.340
I'm sure we all want to know 
exactly who Shimano is,

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.700
but right now, we have more pressing issues.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.010
Shimano has not only saved the life 
of our fellow countrywoman,

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:37.280
but now, he's also rescued us 
from a life-or-death situation.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:40.670
A-Alain, wait... You can't mean...

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.200
Here's my proposal.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.710
From now on, we consider Shimano an ally,

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:49.080
and formally ask for his help

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:51.790
in our upcoming operations.

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:57.510
Alain, have you lost your mind?!

00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:59.970
Marie, what do you think?

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:05.130
I agree with you.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:09.210
Damn it, you always take his side.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.520
There you have it.

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.660
Shimano, will you lend us,

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.720
and our motherland, your aid?

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:26.040
Before I answer, let's get away from here.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:28.550
The enemy's still nearby.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:32.880
Where's your closest hideout?

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.060
Châtillon.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:36.440
It'll take an hour to get there on foot.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:37.550
Let's go.

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:42.870
Stop.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:47.760
Marie?

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:50.480
What's wrong?

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:52.570
Why are you pointing the gun at us?

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.270
I'm sorry.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:58.540
If only he hadn't shown up, 
this never would've...

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.770
I thought so.

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:04.200
I knew it was a possibility, 
but I see there was a mole after all.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.550
You were the one who sabotaged that gun.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.330
No... You're a collaborator?

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:14.130
A German spy sent to infiltrate the Résistance?

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:15.510
Are you?

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.680
Marie, why?

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.310
Why would you cooperate—

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:20.810
To survive!

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:26.070
So that my family and I could...

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.670
It was a month ago.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:42.930
I was going to keep up the act to the end.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:47.380
I did everything in my power 
not to put you all in danger.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:49.090
But...

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:01.360
If this keeps up, my family will...

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:03.830
I'm truly sorry.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.860
But I have to hand you over to the Germans.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.680
Both of you, and the strange Japanese man.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:16.810
Wha— Stop!

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:18.490
I'll shoot!

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:20.550
Please, stop!

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:22.570
Come any closer and...

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:25.270
Shoot, then.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.030
What's wrong?

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:30.880
Don't die.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:35.610
And...

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.180
<i>Don't kill!</i>

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.950
Jean, got anything to tie her up with?

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.120
Hey. Are you awake?

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.170
Yeah...

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.970
So Marie chose her family over her country,

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:12.700
and Jean chose the love of his life.

00:17:15.850 --> 00:17:18.380
"A man like that for friends beloved,

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:21.100
or for his country fears not to perish."

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.020
It's easier said than done.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:29.150
How did you know Marie was a collaborator?

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.670
She asked me what was wrong.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:34.960
After I repaired the gun,

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:38.110
she was the only one who asked 
what was wrong with it.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:41.860
She was trying to discern if I'd noticed that

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:43.360
it had been tampered with.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:47.470
I wasn't 100% sure, though,

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:49.880
which is why I put a blank in it.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:57.430
What I didn't predict was the shot Jean took.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.650
But thanks to him,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.430
I remembered who I am,

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:04.310
and what I have to do.

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:06.730
Are you leaving?

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:07.730
Yes.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.740
I see.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.300
Alain Lernier.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.130
That's my name.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:23.740
Shimano Ryousuke.

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.540
<i>Cleaning's complete.</i>

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:44.060
<i>No witnesses, no listening devices.</i>

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:48.140
<i>Contact protocol number three.</i>

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:09.580
Report.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.360
<i>Why is Lieutenant Colonel Yuuki here?</i>

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.030
Ninety to eight to two.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:23.520
That's the current ratio of bystanders,

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:25.490
to German collaborators,

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:27.950
to Résistance here in France.

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:32.600
<i>The purpose of my mission was 
Alain's group from the start.</i>

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:36.820
<i>We knew from prior intel 
that Alain was a leader</i>

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:38.730
<i>within the Résistance movement.</i>

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:41.990
<i>In order to infiltrate their group,</i>

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:43.720
<i>I gave the old lady a suggestion</i>

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:45.960
<i>and sent her over to the Germans.</i>

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:50.320
<i>Alain and the others coming to my 
rescue was all according to plan.</i>

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.780
<i>Getting hit in the head during the confusion</i>

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:58.000
<i>and experiencing temporary 
amnesia was a blunder,</i>

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.740
<i>but it was a miscalculation within 
the realm of possibility,</i>

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:02.590
<i>and thus, something I planned for.</i>

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:06.800
It should be easy for a spy to see that, 
in less than ideal situations,

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:10.140
the potential for confusion 
and memory loss can arise.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.500
However, once you master
the method of imprinting

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:17.730
your mission objective into your subconscious,

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.050
it won't cause any problems.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:23.540
At present, the French Résistance is mostly

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:27.200
students causing occasional problems.

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:33.050
I see no evidence of an organized 
system supplying arms to them.

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:36.520
So, what next?

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:38.750
Shall I continue a bit longer?

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:40.860
Return home for now.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:45.210
The next Hakusan Maru voyage 
will be the last one.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:48.630
<i>The last voyage?</i>

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:50.310
<i>It can't be...</i>

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:55.390
<i>Japan is allying with Germany?</i>

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.750
<i>I can't imagine Germany has a clear 
vision for how they'll control</i>

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:04.750
<i>the seas and the skies as 
they battle the British.</i>

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:08.350
<i>They just declared war on them the other day.</i>

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:10.640
<i>So why...</i>

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.410
<i>This was the</i>

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:17.280
<i>biggest miscalculation I made on this mission.</i>

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:19.870
<i>Mission complete.</i>

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:25.530
<i>I can't use the mask of Shimano Ryousuke, 
the exchange student, anymore.</i>

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:30.150
<i>Lieutenant Colonel Yuuki's 
presence here confirms that.</i>

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:39.030
You're welcome to stay.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:41.650
I'll go home.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:43.070
But...

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.960
Next time, give me a mission that 
demands a little more mettle.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.960
<b>Preview</b>

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:26.500
You've been really into 
gambling lately, haven't you?

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:27.670
Yeah.

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:28.880
Why?

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:31.350
To taste the thrill of victory,

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:34.880
Episode 4 - City of Temptation

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:34.270
and the agony of defeat.
